---
title: The Zone of Interest
subject: dir. Jonathan Glazer
genre: Film
rating: 4
date: 2026-07-09
excerpt: >-
  The horror is entirely off-screen and entirely inescapable. A garden wall doing
  the work of a thousand cuts.
---

A family tends its garden. There is a greenhouse, a pool, a wall, and on the other side of the wall a sound design that never stops. Glazer's decision to keep the camera on the domestic side of that wall for the entire runtime is the most rigorous formal choice in recent cinema, and it makes the film almost unwatchable in a way no depiction could.

The cameras are locked off and unmanned, placed in the house like surveillance. No coverage, no close-ups to tell you how to feel, no score in the conventional sense — Mica Levi's sound is closer to a pressure change. What you get instead is the ordinary texture of a promotion, a birthday, a mother-in-law's visit, and a distant mechanical hum that you stop hearing about forty minutes in. That is the film's actual subject: how quickly you stop hearing it.

> The flowers are doing well this year.

Hüller and Friedel play the Hösses without a single moment of interiority offered to the audience. There is no scene where a conscience flickers. Glazer withholds that satisfaction on principle, because the point is not that these people were monsters but that they were administrators with a nice house.

It loses a little force in the final act, where a coda leaps forward in time and states out loud what the preceding ninety minutes had made you feel without a word. A rare misstep in a film otherwise built entirely out of what it refuses to show.
